!!<*yamlover: $defs: chapter>
Anchors
description: the push — & gives this node another real path
- |
  `&path` **pushes**: "I *also* live there". An anchor's name is a full pointer path —
  same ladder, same metacharacters, same escaping — and declaring `&: P: k` on a node
  means the container at `: P` gains the entry `k`: a ref edge to this node, exactly
  what a forward `k: *me` authored there would produce; `normalize` folds the two
  authorings of one edge together. The authored position stays the containment spine
  and the node's identity path.
- !!<format: text/x-yamlover> |
  humans:
    - age: 30
      pet: &: supercat    # ALSO the document-root key "supercat"
        species: cat
    - age: 10
      pet: *: supercat    # a plain path — no anchor namespace
- |
  **Anchors are real keys, not a namespace** — `*name` is pure path lookup; there is
  no precedence rule and no local-versus-global ambiguity. **Anchors are not
  entries**: they never count toward a node's kind — a scalar with anchors is still a
  scalar, a blob still a blob; no tag is needed to anchor a scalar. An anchor attaches
  to a node; a pointer entry (`k: *p`) cannot carry one.
- |
  **Multiplicity and placement.** A node may declare any number of anchors: on the
  value's line (quoted when colon-styled — `path: &': another: path' 12`), or — the
  canonical style — on their own lines after the value, where the token runs to end
  of line unquoted. A whole-document scalar is tagged in two bare lines:
- !!<format: text/x-yamlover> |
  30
  &:: tags: field: mathematics: numbers: whole[]
- |
  **Ordinal anchors.** A trailing `[]` makes the membership keyless: the container
  gains this node as a positional member. No position may be claimed — `&: path: 3`
  is rejected (a bare-integer tail is a position); order is the container's own data
  ([order is data](::language:principles:order-is-data)) — and membership is
  **additive**: each `[]` appends one element, lists may repeat — except into a
  `!!set`, where duplicates collapse by target. Anchor-created members project after
  the container's own entries, lexicographically by member path. Anchor paths must be
  **unambiguous**: wildcards and matchers are rejected by the arity rule, position
  claims by this one. A keyed anchor must end in a nonempty *string* key — it may not
  create the null key either (`&: p: ~` is rejected; the null key is authored in
  place, [null keys](::language:vs-yaml:null-keys)); the literal-tilde key is fine,
  quoted (`&: p: '~'`).
- |
  **Collisions.** An anchor-created entry may meet an authored one at the same path —
  valid exactly when the two denote the same thing: the same target node, or
  structurally equal values. Unequal declarations are a **reported conflict**,
  surfaced like dangling pointers, never silently dropped. In-document anchors are
  checked at parse time; cross-document anchors (project and world scopes) are
  realized and checked at index time.
- |
  Relocation needs no special form — point a new place at the existing node with `*`
  (`acting_boss: *: pets: 0`). And the deprecated tilde spellings read as anchors
  through the migration window — `~key: *P` ≡ `&: P: key`, `~- *P` ≡ `&: P[]`
  ([the tilde sigil](::language:vs-yaml:tilde)).
